---
import ExtensionSpring from '@/components/diagrams/ExtensionSpring.astro';
import MaterialsTable from '@/components/ui/MaterialsTable.astro';
import Todo from '@/components/ui/Todo.astro';
import { extension } from '@/data/products';
import ProductLayout from '@/layouts/ProductLayout.astro';
---

<ProductLayout product={extension}>
  <Fragment slot="about">
    <p>
      An extension spring — also called a tension spring — is a <strong>close-wound helix</strong>{' '}
      with a hook or loop at each end. It resists a pull. Most are made with
      <strong>initial tension</strong>: a preload that keeps the coils shut until the load exceeds
      that threshold.
    </p>
    <p>
      Specify <strong>length inside hooks</strong>, not only overall length. Two springs with the
      same body can fit very differently once the hooks are bent. Also specify the angle between the
      hooks (in line, or at 90 degrees).
    </p>
    <p>
      The hook is the weak point. Failures start at the inner radius of the bend, not in the body. A
      machine loop with a generous radius lasts longer than a sharp crossover. If the spring has
      been breaking in service, send the failed part — the fracture face usually tells us it was the
      hook.
    </p>
    <p>
      Wire {extension.specs[0]?.value}, same material list as our other springs. We do not grind
      extension springs; there is nothing to stand flat.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="types">
    <ul class="grid gap-5 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <li class="type-fig">
        <div class="bp-grid type-draw">
          <ExtensionSpring hook="machine-loop" decorative />
        </div>
        <p>
          <strong>Machine loop.</strong> A full loop bent back onto the body. The usual strong end.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="type-fig">
        <div class="bp-grid type-draw">
          <ExtensionSpring hook="extended-hook" decorative />
        </div>
        <p>
          <strong>Extended hook.</strong> The hook stands off the body so you can reach a distant
          pin.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-[46rem]">
      Side loops, reduced eyes and 0°/90° combinations we will read off the drawing.
      <Todo text="list the hook types you actually form." />
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <MaterialsTable slot="materials" />

  <Fragment slot="tips">
    <p>
      Write <strong>length inside hooks</strong> and the hook style on the drawing. If you only have
      a sample, measure the body length and say “machine loop each end, in line” or attach a photo
      with a ruler.
    </p>
    <p>
      Initial tension matters when the hook must not rattle at rest. If you do not know the number,
      describe the job: “must stay shut until the lever moves” is enough for a first pass.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="applications">
    <p>
      Return springs on levers, latches and small linkages; furniture recliners; switch mechanisms;
      instrumentation. Automotive assemblies in our wire range — throttle, clutch and stand-return
      layouts — use this spring constantly.
    </p>
    <p>
      We do not make trampoline, garage-door or rolling-shutter extension springs. Those are a
      different factory and a different wire.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ProductLayout>

<style>
  .type-fig {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .type-draw {
    padding: 16px;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-grid);
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
    color: var(--color-navy-900);
  }

  .type-fig p {
    margin-top: 10px;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }
</style>
